TI  - Evaluation of Hemodynamic and Arterial Stifness Parameters in Women with Htais/Preeclampsia
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF SOGR 2018: THE 17TH NATIONAL CONGRESS OF THE ROMANIAN SOCIETY OF OBSTETRICS AND GYNECOLOGY & FIRST ADVANCED COLPOSCOPY COURSE
CP  - 17th National Congress of the Romanian-Society-of-Obstetrics-and-Gynecology / 1st Advanced Colposcopy Course
AB  - Arterial stiffness results from a degenerative process that mainly affects extracellular matrix of the elastic arteries, under the effect of aging and the risk factors. Among the markers of cardiovascular disease, arterial stiffness has been shown to be an important parameter for the assessing cardiovascular risk. Physical activity during pregnancy offers many health benefits. Physical activity during pregnancy is associated with a reduced risk of preeclampsia, gestational diabetes or preterm birth.
   Material and methods
   We studied the incidence of hemodynamic parameters for arterial stiffness during pregnancy and after pregnancy in 100 female patients hospitalized in the Bega Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology of the Emergency County Hospital, Timisoara; some patients developed cardiovascular disease. For this study we used the Arteriograph Medexpert and studied the following parameters: PWV, PP, cardiac frequency (CF), systolic blood pressure (SBP) and diastolic blood pressure (DBP). Statistical analysis was performed with specialized programs EPI INFO for t-tests, respectively Paired t-test and Unpaired t-test.
   Results and discussion
   Physical training coordinated by the obstetrician, cardiologist and a kinesiotherapist improves hemodynamic parameters (SBP, PP, CF) in pregnant women during pregnancy.
   Physical activity during pregnancy can have good implications for psychological health, including the mood and self-esteem.
   Conclusions
   Physical activity remains an important measure in preventing cardiovascular disease, and brings important benefits for pregnant women. Some of the conditions experienced by newborns have a higher incidence in mothers who had no physical activity during pregnancy.
